  "worked_solution": "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}\u2061({x}^{2}+4) & =\\operatorname{log}\u2061(2x+4) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =2x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}\u2061({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}\u2061(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}\u2061({0}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}\u2061(2(0)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}\u2061(4) & =\\operatorname{log}\u2061(4).\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}\u2061({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}\u2061(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}\u2061({2}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}\u2061(2(2)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}\u2061(8) & =\\operatorname{log}\u2061(8).\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=0,2.$",
